WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
The alternatives that usually win.

2
00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:06.936
When the four questions say no, or say “not yet”, pick from these, simplest first.

3
00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:08.560
Deterministic code.

4
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:14.941
If part of the task is parsing, lookup, arithmetic, date resolution, or formatting, do it in code.

5
00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:17.845
It is free, instant, and right every time.

6
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:25.933
The best agent designs push everything deterministic out of the model, as the invoice example in the knowledge check below shows.

7
00:00:26.213 --> 00:00:28.283
A single call with better context.

8
00:00:28.563 --> 00:00:32.893
Many “we need an agent” proposals are really “the model lacks information”.

9
00:00:33.173 --> 00:00:43.456
Supply it: retrieve the relevant documents (Studio 2), include a few worked examples, put the facts in the prompt, and ask for structured output you can validate.

10
00:00:43.736 --> 00:00:46.915
Anthropic’s post makes this the default starting point.

11
00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:48.112
A workflow.

12
00:00:48.392 --> 00:00:57.117
If the task has steps, a chain with gates, a router, or an evaluator-optimizer loop gives you most of the benefit with control you can test.

13
00:00:57.397 --> 00:00:59.061
The lab this week builds one.

14
00:00:59.341 --> 00:01:01.069
An agent with a narrow scope.

15
00:01:01.349 --> 00:01:13.147
If the task really is open-ended, shrink it: read-only tools first, a strict step and cost budget, a human approval on every write, and a workflow around it that handles the common cases.

16
00:01:13.427 --> 00:01:16.477
Grow its autonomy as evals and transcripts earn it.

17
00:01:16.757 --> 00:01:18.656
Notice what the list has in common.

18
00:01:18.936 --> 00:01:23.096
Each option moves a decision from the model to something you can verify.

19
00:01:23.376 --> 00:01:25.744
That is the habit this week is meant to build.
